/etc/config/lltd - LLTD responder (Windows network map, LLTD mappers)
=====================================================================
UCI files lose their comments when LuCI or "uci commit" writes them, so the
explanations live here.

lltd answers Link Layer Topology Discovery (MS-LLTD) on the given networks:
the device appears in the Windows network map (and in LLTD mappers such as
LLTD-Netzwerkuebersicht) with name, IP, medium and speed, and takes part in
the topology tests.

config lltd 'main'
  enabled        '1' = run lltd
  interface      logical networks (list), default 'lan'. Never put WAN here.
  hostname       machine name to report (max. 16 characters), default:
                 system host name
  friendly_name  longer display name, default: board model
                 (/tmp/sysinfo/model)
  icon           own icon file (ICO, PNG, GIF or JPEG, max. 32 KiB);
                 default: built-in router icon
  web            '1' = announce the web interface (http://<ip>/), Windows
                 then offers "View device webpage"
  nat_private    '1' = this interface is the private side of a NAT router,
                 '0' = not, 'auto' (default) = '1' if a network 'wan' exists
  fdb            '1' (default) = add the source address of Train frames to
                 the bridge FDB as a local address for a few seconds, so that
                 test frames for it reach this device instead of being
                 flooded. Needed on bridges (br-lan), especially with DSA
                 switches; without it topology rounds through this device
                 become invalid. '0' = off.
  fdb_hold       how long such an entry stays, in ms (default 5000)
  speed          link speed to report in Mbit/s (default: detected; for a
                 bridge the fastest member port)
  verbose        0 = errors/warnings, 1 = info, 2 = debug, 3 = frame dump
                 (log: logread -e lltd)

Requires root/CAP_NET_RAW (raw socket on the interface); while a mapper
runs a topology test the interface is in promiscuous mode.
